A||||3||2FAST4U||Motor/Trans Mounts||||1||
Z||000000||2FAST4U||12-16-1999||01:09 AM||||I've allways worked on my 4-cyl fieros from changing oil to rebuilding my engines but I just bought an 85 GT with a 6 and manual trans. Since the dog bone is completely shot ( doesn't even have any rubber in it) I'm sure it will need motor/trans mounts also. Any tips on changing them on a 6 would be appreciated to help the job go easier and faster. Also where is the best place to buy the for fast service. My local Chevy dealer takes for ever to get parts.<P>TIA<P>Joe||||reg||1
Z||000001||Carrolles||12-16-1999||07:16 AM||||You can get the mounts at AutoZone.<BR>The mounts may not be shot.  I replaced a bad dogbone a few years ago.  Recently I had the engine out and looked at the engine/trans mounts and they were all fine.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||dogtired||12-16-1999||10:04 AM||||Check it out good before you go buy a bunch of stuff. On one I purchased not too long ago I thought the same thing, Dogbone shot, so everything must be. WRONG. The little shock looking motor mount (beside the starter) was not hooked up (upper bolt gone) I replaced the bolt and dogbone and everything else is COOL  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Been There, Done That. GOOD LUCK||||reg||6
Z||000003||deceler8||12-16-1999||11:12 AM||||If all the rubber is gone from your dogbone, you're a perfect candidate for a poly rebush kit. Don't buy from the Fiero store, go to <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com" TARGET=_blank>www.suspension.com</A>  and order the bushings from them. $17 last time I looked. <P>They slip right in once all the old rubber is gone. Since yours is an '85, you shouldn't have to deal with the "ribbed" problem.<P>15 minute job once you get the bushings...no kidding.||||reg||10
